<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pechora River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pechora</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pechora is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-russia">European Russia</a>, about 1,800 kilometres long, rising in the northern Urals and flowing north into the Barents Sea. Its basin was a source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> for medieval Novgorod, which collected tribute from the region's peoples from the eleventh century. The lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> with the fall of Novgorod, and the region later became important for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and oil.</p></div>
